Karuikey includes source code, keyboard resources, and a narrow read-only native
dictionary bridge derived from the Android Open Source Project LatinIME keyboard.
The derived files are materially modified for Karuikey and retain Apache License
2.0 notices. The imported foundation covers keyboard geometry, resource parsing,
hit detection, keyboard selection, shift state, gesture path sampling, the
alphabetic/numeric/phone layouts, and the AOSP binary-dictionary query core.
Karuikey loads only one active-language dictionary at a time. Autocorrect,
telemetry, and remote services are not included.

The Java/native dictionary bridge is derived from AOSP LatinIME at commit
127336e9f29d69607eab55982324b210279ae8c5:
https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/inputmethods/LatinIME/
The standalone ndk-build files and the registered JNI surface are Karuikey
adaptations. AOSP's java/NOTICE mentions Lexiteria dictionaries; no Lexiteria
dictionary data is redistributed here.

Dictionary data
---------------
Karuikey bundles the standard English (US) and experimental Russian binary dictionaries
from the Helium314 aosp-dictionaries repository at commit
55e6d1c64ad72481d5615113b1c94f0716617016:
https://codeberg.org/Helium314/aosp-dictionaries
The corresponding source manifests are wordlists/main_en_US.source and
wordlists_experimental/main_ru.source. The English manifest points to the OpenBoard v1.4.5
word list; retain the applicable OpenBoard license and attribution when redistributing it.
The Russian manifest identifies its source lists as licensed under the Creative Commons
Attribution 4.0 International license:
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
The English source is the OpenBoard v1.4.5 word list referenced by the source manifest. The
Russian source is derived from Leipzig Corpora Collection downloads. Karuikey redistributes the
binary dictionary files without modification and preserves this attribution. The English
dictionary contains 160,715 words and next-word data; the Russian experimental dictionary
contains 1,541,848 words and next-word data, as reported by the source repository.
The resulting binary dictionaries are distributed as
app/src/main/assets/dictionaries/main_en_us.dict (SHA-256
bd950ef4b57655120eee65cee62a5d216a63f721d9a8bb759ce2022437840443) and
main_ru.dict (SHA-256
806243bb3b799ef89c3aa1a147b5d12d2ec19271c6b3c0bbff7147af14c14c01).
Attribution and the license link are retained here. These files are not the
AOSP bundled Lexiteria dictionaries and no Lexiteria data is bundled.

Additional keyboard layout resources
------------------------------------
The Greek, Bulgarian, Serbian QWERTZ, Colemak, and Dvorak layout closures were adapted from
the Android Open Source Project LatinIME repository at commit
127336e9f29d69607eab55982324b210279ae8c5:
https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/inputmethods/LatinIME/
The imported files are the corresponding keyboard_layout_set_*.xml, kbd_*.xml, rows_*.xml,
rowkeys_*.xml files and keys_dvorak_123.xml under app/src/main/res/xml. Their AOSP custom
resource namespace was changed to Android's standalone res-auto namespace; the layout data and
Apache 2.0 headers are retained. These files are marked as Karuikey adaptations. No HeliBoard
code or keyboard assets are redistributed in this build; HeliBoard was used only for source
comparison.

Emoji data
----------
assets/emoji/emoji.tsv is generated by tools/emoji/generate_emoji.py from the Unicode
emoji-test.txt (Emoji 16.0, https://unicode.org/Public/emoji/16.0/) and the CLDR 48.0.0
annotations and derived annotations for English and Russian
(https://github.com/unicode-org/cldr-json). Copyright © 1991-2025 Unicode, Inc.
Licensed under the Unicode License v3: https://www.unicode.org/license.txt

